ee are positions 4,5,6. But since J is in position 4, which is after lunch, that would mean the first three are before lunch, and the last three are after. So, positions 1-3 before lunch, 4-6 after.\n\nBut wait, condition 3 says G must speak before lunch, so G is in positions 1-3.\n\nNow, let's consider the conditions:\n\n- F is in position 1 or 3.\n\n- Only one person is between M and N. So, M and N are separated by one person. So, if M is in position x, N is in x+2.\n\n- G is in positions 1-3.\n\nLet's try to construct the order.\n\nSince J is in position 4, which is after lunch, the first three are before lunch, and the last three are after. So, positions 1-3 before lunch, 4-6 after.\n\nNow, let's consider the possible positions for F and G.\n\nSince F is in position 1 or 3, and G is in positions 1-3.\n\nLet's try to place F first. Suppose F is in position 1. Then, G must be in positions 2 or 3, since G must be before lunch, which is positions 1-3.\n\nBut wait, if F is in position 1, then G could be in position 2 or 3.\n\nAlternatively, if F is in position 3, then G could be in position 1 or 2.\n\nLet's try to see.\n\nSince J is in position 4, which is after lunch, the last three positions (4-6) are after lunch. So, positions 4,5,6 are after lunch.\n\nNow, let's consider the speakers: F, G, J, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M, N. Wait, no, the six are F, G, J, L, M,