e the order.\n\nWe have M2 > M1 from above.\n\nWe need to find the order of P, E, M1, M2.\n\nWe know that P > E.\n\nWe also know that M2 = P + M1 - E\n\nBut perhaps it's better to assign some hypothetical values to make it clearer.\n\nLet's assume some values that satisfy the conditions.\n\nLet's assume E = 10.\n\nThen, since P > E, let's say P = 12.\n\nFrom condition 1: P + M1 = E + M2 \u2192 12 + M1 = 10 + M2 \u2192 M2 = M1 + 2\n\nFrom condition 3: M1 > P + E \u2192 M1 > 12 + 10 \u2192 M1 > 22\n\nSo M1 must be greater than 22.\n\nThen, M2 = M1 + 2, so M2 > 24.\n\nNow, let's check condition 2: P + M2 > E + M1 \u2192 12 + M2 > 10 + M1\n\nBut since M2 = M1 + 2, substitute:\n\n12 + M1 + 2 > 10 + M1 \u2192 14 + M1 > 10 + M1 \u2192 14 > 10, which is true.\n\nSo the conditions are satisfied.\n\nNow, let's see the scores:\n\nE = 10\n\nP = 12\n\nM1 > 22\n\nM2 = M1 + 2 > 24\n\nSo the scores are:\n\nM2 > M1 > P > E\n\nWait, but let's check the order.\n\nWe have M2 > M1, and M1 > P (since P=12 and M1>22), so M2 > M1 > P > E.\n\nBut wait, let's see the options given:\n\nOptions are:\n\nA) Major subject one, major subject two, english, politics.\n\nB) Major subject two, major subject one, politics, english.\n\nC) Major subject one, major subject two, politics, english.\n\nD) Major subject two, major subject one, english, politics.\n\nWait, perhaps the options are about the order of the four subjects: major one, major two, politics, English.\n\nSo let's see the scores:\n\nWe have M2 > M1 > P > E.\n\nBut wait, perhaps the order is based on the subjects, not the scores.\n\nWait, perhaps the question is asking for the ranking of the four subjects (politics, English, major one, major two) from high to low.\n\nSo let's think in terms of the subjects, not their scores.\n\nWe need to determine which subject has the highest score, then the next, etc.\n\nFrom our earlier analysis, we have:\n\nM2 > M1 > P > E\n\nBut let's see the options:\n\nOption B: Major subject two, major subject one,