s extreme as before, so sea level might not rise as much. But the expert's opinion is about reversing the trend, not about the magnitude. So this might not directly affect the reversal.\n\nSo the key here is that the experts are estimating that the trend will reverse. To weaken this, we need an option that suggests that the trend won't reverse or that the experts' estimate is incorrect.\n\nOption A says snowfall has decreased, which would mean less added water. If the added water isn't enough to offset the ice melting, then the trend might not reverse. So this would weaken the expert's opinion.\n\nOption B says the increase in ice from snow is only one-third of the ice loss. So maybe the added water isn't enough to reverse the trend. But I'm not sure if this is as strong as option A.\n\nOption D is about temperature, which might affect sea level differently, but it's more about the magnitude rather than the trend.\n\nSo I think the best answer is A because if snowfall has decreased, the added water might not be enough to reverse the trend.\n</think>\n\nThe option that would most weaken the expert's opinion is:\n\n**A) According to relevant statistics, the amount of snowfall in Antarctica has slightly decreased in recent years.**\n\nThis would suggest that the added frozen water from snowfall is insufficient to reverse the trend, thus weakening the expert's optimistic view.\n\n**Answer: A**",
