      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out the assumption in the researcher's argument. Let me read the context again. The researcher says that since most recaptured armadillos were within a few hundred yards of where they were tagged last spring, he concluded they don't move into new territories. \n\nThe question is asking which of the options is an assumption required by this argument. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nFirst, I should understand the researcher's reasoning. He observed that recaptured armadillos were near the tagged area, suggesting they didn't move much. So he inferred that they don't move rapidly into new territories.\n\nNow, to find the assumption, I need to think about what the researcher is making an assumption about. The researcher is making a conclusion based on the observed proximity of recaptured armadillos to the tagged area. So the assumption would be that if the armadillos did move into new territories, they would have been further away from the tagged area.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) A large majority of the recaptured armadillos did not move to a new territory in the intervening summer and then move back to the old territory by the fall. This seems like a strong assumption because if they did move, they would have been further away, which would contradict the researcher's conclusion. So this is likely the assumption.\n\nB) Predators did not kill any of the armadillos that had been tagged the previous spring. This is more about the survival of the tagged individuals, but the researcher's argument is about movement, not survival. So this might not be the assumption.\n\nC) The tags identifying the armadillos cannot be removed by the armadillos, either by accident or deliberately. This is about the tags being unique, but again, the researcher's argument is about movement, not about the tags themselves. So maybe not the assumption.\n\nD) Of the armadillos living in the area of the tagging site last spring, few were able to avoid being tagged by the researcher. This is about the initial population, but again, the researcher's argument is about movement, not about the initial capture. So perhaps not the assumption.\n\nSo the main assumption is that if the armadillos moved, they would have been further away, which would mean the researcher's observation supports the idea that they don't move. Therefore, option A is the assumption.\n</think>\n\nThe assumption required by the researcher's argument is that if the armadillos had moved into new territories, they would have been further away from the tagged area. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n**A)** A large majority of the recaptured armadillos did not move to a new territory in the intervening summer and then move back to the old territory by the fall.",
