y one person is telling the truth, then A is true, and others are false. So, if A is true, then C is false, which is consistent. But does this necessarily mean that there is a pen in the second drawer? Yes, because if A is true, then C is false, meaning the pen is in the second drawer. So, if A is true, then option A is true.\n\nBut wait, let's check if this leads to any contradictions.\n\nIf A is true, then some drawers don't have books. B is false, so not all have books. C is false, so pen in second. D is false, so no letters in third.\n\nNow, let's see if this is possible.\n\nSuppose the second drawer has a pen (C is false, so no pen in second). The third drawer has no letters (D is false). The first and fourth drawers could have books or not.\n\nBut since A is true, not all drawers have books. So, perhaps the first and fourth have books, and the second doesn't, but wait, the second has a pen, so it's not a book. So, perhaps the first and fourth have books, and the second has a pen, third has nothing, and maybe the fourth has something else.\n\nBut perhaps this is getting too detailed. Let's consider another approach.\n\nSince only one person is telling the truth, let's consider each possibility where only one statement is true, and see what that implies.\n\nLet's consider each case where only one statement is true, and see what the implications are.\n\nCase 1: Only A is true.\n\nSo, A is true: There are no books in some drawers. So, not all drawers have books. Therefore, at least one has a book.\n\nB is false: There are not books in all drawers. So, at least one has a book, which is consistent.\n\nC is false: There is a pen in the second drawer. So, second drawer has a pen.\n\nD is false: There are no letters in the third drawer. So, third drawer has letters.\n\nNow, let's see what this implies about the drawers.\n\nWe know that the second drawer has a pen (C is false). The third drawer has letters (D is false). The first and fourth drawers could have books or n