buy more because they have saved up, that would weaken Li Ming's argument because it suggests that people can still buy more despite low prices, meaning the economy might still improve.\n\nSo looking at option B: If consumers still believe that the economy will improve eventually, then they won't be able to buy more because they expect the economy to get better. This would weaken Li Ming's argument because it suggests that people won't buy more even if prices are low because they expect the economy to improve.\n\nOption D says that in bad times, people might cut back on spending because they're not employed, but when prices hit rock bottom, they can save. This would mean that people might still buy what they can save, which would support Li Ming's argument that prices hitting rock bottom won't help. So this would strengthen Li Ming's argument, not weaken it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B because it suggests that consumers won't buy more despite low prices because they expect the economy to improve, thus weakening Li Ming's rebuttal.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is **B**. This option suggests that consumers believe the economy will improve, meaning they won't buy more despite low prices, thus weakening Li Ming's argument that prices hitting rock bottom won't help the economy.",
