.\nThis would mean that Loux knows she has to sell the stoke farm, but the argument is that selling it would help. If Loux is aware, maybe she's more likely to sell it, but this doesn't necessarily weaken the argument that selling it would help. It might support it, but perhaps not weaken it. So maybe this is a possible answer.\n\nD) Loux's grandson had repeatedly expressed his desire to own a farm.\nThis would mean that the grandson wants to own a farm, so perhaps the estate's remainder would go to him, not to Zembaty. This would weaken the argument because it suggests that the remainder would go to the grandson, not to Zembaty selling the farm. So this would make the solicitor's argument weaker.\n\nWait, let's think again. The original argument is that selling the stoke farm would help clear debts, so the solicitor's argument is that selling it would be acceptable. Now, if the grandson wants to own a farm, perhaps the remainder would go to him, meaning that Zembaty wouldn't have to sell the farm. So this would weaken the argument because it suggests that the remainder would go to the grandson, not to Zembaty selling it. Therefore, this would make the solicitor's argument weaker.\n\nSo the options are B and D. Let's see:\n\nOption B says the debts couldn't have been cleared without selling the stoke farm. This would support the solicitor's argument because it would mean that selling it is necessary. So it strengthens the argument, not weaken.\n\nOption D says the grandson wants to own a farm, so perhaps the remainder would go to him, meaning that Zembaty wouldn't have to sell the farm. This would weaken the argument because it suggests that the remainder would go to the grandson, not to Zembaty selling it. Therefore, this would make the solicitor's argument weaker.\n\nSo the correct answer is D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is D.\n\n**Explanation:**\nThe solicitor's argument is that selling the stoke farm would help clear debts. Option D states that Loux's grandson wanted to own a farm, suggesting that the remainder would go to him instead of Zembaty selling it. This would weaken the argument because it implies the remainder would go to the grandson, not to Zembaty selling the farm. Therefore, D weakens the solicitor's position.",
