n\nA) Older children are more likely to have false memories than younger ones. This aligns with the context because older participants showed more false memory errors.\n\nB) Older and younger children make different mistakes when remembering. This might be true, but the context specifically mentions older participants having more false memory errors, not necessarily different mistakes. It's possible that both groups have errors, but the focus is on older having more.\n\nC) Children testify more reliably than teenagers or adults. The context doesn't mention anything about reliability, just about false memory. So this might not be correct.\n\nD) Depending on the nature of different events and evidence and testimony, young children may sometimes remember more accurately. The context doesn't mention anything about reliability, just about false memory. So this might not be correct either.\n\nGiven the context, the most directly relevant point is that older participants were more likely to develop different types of false memory. Therefore, option A is the best answer.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**A) Older children are more likely to have false memories than younger ones**\n\nThis directly aligns with the context where older participants showed more false memory errors.",
