oving it is safer, but this doesn't directly address the argument about removing all.\n\nSo the key here is that the argument is about removing all causing potential health issues. To strengthen this, we need to show that removing all is less likely to cause issues. Option B says that removed asbestos is often forgotten, so it's less likely to be disturbed again. That would mean less fibers released, thus less risk. So this would strengthen the argument.\n\nOption C is about individual risk without protective gear, which might support the argument that removing all is safer, but it's more about individual cases rather than the overall argument about removing all.\n\nTherefore, the best answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**B) Much of the time, removed asbestos is buried in landfills and forgotten, with no guarantee that it will not be disturbed again.**\n\nThis option supports the argument by indicating that removing all asbestos is less likely to cause health issues because the fibers are less likely to be released again.",
