      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which principle explains the contrast in voters' reactions based on the given context. Let me break down the context first.\n\nThe context says that when a major political scandal happens before an election and voters blame it equally on all parties, most incumbents are returned to office. But if voters blame the scandal on only one party, then incumbents from that party are likely defeated by challengers from other parties. Also, the proportion of incumbents seeking re-election is high and constant.\n\nThe question is about which principle explains this contrast. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me analyze each option.\n\nOption A: \"Major political scandals can practically always be blamed on incumbents, but whether those incumbents should be voted out of office depends on who their challengers are.\" Hmm, this seems to suggest that the blame is on incumbents, but their re-election depends on their own challengers. That might explain why some incumbents are defeated if their own party's challengers win, but others might be re-elected if their own party's challengers lose. But I'm not sure if this directly explains the high proportion of incumbents seeking re-election.\n\nOption B: \"When a major political scandal is blamed on incumbents from all parties, that judgment is more accurate than any judgment that incumbents from only one party are to blame.\" This seems to suggest that blaming all parties equally is more accurate than blaming just one. So, if the scandal is blamed on all parties equally, the incidence is more accurate, meaning that the incumbents are more likely to be re-elected because their own party's challengers win. But the context says that when the scandal is blamed on all parties equally, most incumbents are re-elected, which might be because their own party's challengers win. So this could explain why the proportion is high when all parties are equally blamed.\n\nOption C: \"When major political scandals are less the responsibility of individual incumbents than of the parties to which they belong, whatever party was responsible must be penalized when possible.\" This seems to suggest that if the scandal is more about the party than the individual, then the party's members should be penalized. But the context doesn't mention penalizing incumbents; it's more about their re-election. So maybe this isn't the best fit.\n\nOption D: \"Incumbents who are rightly blamed for a major political scandal should not seek\ufffd-election, but if they do, they should not be returned to office.\" This seems to suggest that if an incumbent is correctly blamed, they shouldn't run again. But the context says that when the scandal is blamed on all parties equally, most incumbents are re-elected. So maybe this isn't directly explaining the contrast.\n\nNow, focusing back on the context: when the scandal is blamed equally, most incumbents are re-elected. When it's blamed on one party, fewer incumbents are re-elected because their own party's challengers win. So the principle here is that when the scandal is equally blamed on all parties, the re-election is more likely because the other parties' challengers win. This suggests that the principle is about the proportion of blame and how it affects the outcome.\n\nLooking at option B again: it says that blaming all parties equally is more accurate, meaning that the re-election is more likely because the other parties' challengers win. This aligns with the context because when the scandal is equally blamed, more incumbents are re-elected, but when it's only one party, fewer are. So the principle is that equal blame leads to more re-election, while unequal blame leads to fewer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is B. When a major political scandal is blamed equally on all parties, the incidence is more accurate, leading to more incumbents re-elected as their own party's challengers win. When the scandal is blamed on only one party, fewer incumbents are re-elected because their own party's challengers win. This aligns with the context and the principle that equal blame leads to more re-election.\n\n**Answer: B**",
