ption B says B is on business while A is not, which matches.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the options are:\n\nA) A is on business while B is not\n\nB) B is on business while A is not\n\nC) Both A and B are on business\n\nD) Neither A nor B are on business.\n\nBut since we have B on business and A not, the correct option would be B) B is on business while A is not.\n\nWait, but let's double-check.\n\nFrom judgment 1: A \u2228 B \u2228 C is true. Since C is false, B must be true.\n\nFrom judgment 2: A \u2192 C. Since C is false, A must be false.\n\nTherefore, A is false, B is true, C is false.\n\nSo, looking at the options:\n\nA) A is on business while B is not \u2192 Incorrect, because B is on.\n\nB) B is on business while A is not \u2192 Correct.\n\nC) Both A and B are on business \u2192 Incorrect, because A is not.\n\nD) Neither A nor B are on business \u2192 Incorrect, because B is on.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is option B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is B) B is on business while A is not.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Given Information:**\n   - (1) A \u2228 B \u2228 C is true.\n   - (2) If A is on a business trip, then C is on a business trip (A \u2192 C).\n   - (3) C can't leave because of his busy work, implying C is not on a business trip (C is false).\n\n2. **Analyzing Judgment 3:**\n   - Since C can't leave and is busy, C is not on a business trip \u2192 C is false.\n\n3. **Applying Judgment 2:**\n   - If A is true, then C must be true. But since C is false, A must be false (A \u2192 C is false, so A is false).\n\n4. **Using Judgment 1:**\n   - A \u2228 B \u2228 C is true. Since A is false and C is false, B must be true (B is true).\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   - B is on business, A is not, and C is not.\n\n**Answer:** B) B is on business while A is not.",
