      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to solve this logical reasoning problem. Let's break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context given is about a university administrator's statements regarding funding a new department. The question is asking which of the options (A, B, C, D) logically follows from these statements.\n\nThe context says:\n1. Any proposal for a new department will not be funded if there are fewer than 50 people per year available for hire in that field and the proposed department would duplicate more than 25 percent of the material covered in one of our existing departments.\n2. The proposed Area Studies Department will duplicate more than 25 percent of the material covered in our existing Anthropology Department.\n3. However, we will fund the new department.\n\nSo, the key points are:\n- The new department is funded.\n- Therefore, the conditions that would prevent funding (i.e., fewer than 50 people and duplication over 25%) must not apply here.\n\nLet's rephrase the first statement: If a proposal is made, it won't be funded if two conditions are met:\na) There are fewer than 50 people available for hire in that field.\nb) The proposed department duplicates more than 25% of the material from an existing department.\n\nIn logical terms, the funding condition is: If (fewer than 50) AND (duplicate >25%), then not funded.\n\nBut since the university actually funds the department, that means the conditions (a) and (b) must not both be true. So, either (a) is false, or (b) is false, or both.\n\nNow, looking at the specific case:\n- The proposed department duplicates more than 25% of Anthropology's material. So condition (b) is true.\n- However, the university funds the department. Therefore, condition (a) must be false because if (a) were true, the department would not have been funded.\n\nSo, since the department was funded, and condition (b) is true, condition (a) must be false. Therefore, the field has at least 50 people available for hire.\n\nNow, let's look at the options:\n\nA) If the proposed area studies department did not duplicate more than 25 percent of the material covered in anthropology, then the new department would not be funded.\n- This seems like it's saying that if duplication is \u226425%, then funding is denied. But from the context, we know that duplication is >25%, so this might not be directly applicable. Also, the funding was granted despite duplication, so this might not be correct.\n\nB) The proposed area studies department would not duplicate more than 25 percent of the material covered in any existing department other than anthropology.\n- This seems a bit convoluted. It's saying that the department doesn't duplicate more than 25% in any other department besides Anthropology. But from the context, we know it duplicates more than 25% in Anthropology, so this might not be accurate.\n\nC) The field of area studies has fewer than 50 people per year available for hire.\n- This would mean that the funding condition (a) is true. But since the department was funded, this can't be true because if (a) were true, the department wouldn't have been funded. So this is incorrect.\n\nD) The field of area studies has at least 50 people per year available for hire.\n- This aligns with our earlier conclusion. Since the department was funded, and duplication was >25%, the only way this happened is if the number of available people was at least 50. So this must be correct.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is D.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Understanding the Context:**\n   - The university funds the Area Studies Department despite it duplicating more than 25% of Anthropology's material.\n   - This implies that the funding conditions (fewer than 50 people and duplication >25%) were not met.\n\n2. **Analyzing the Conditions:**\n   - If the department was funded, it means that at least one of the funding conditions was not met.\n   - Since duplication >25% was true, the number of available people must have been \u226550 to avoid funding.\n\n3. **Evaluating the Options:**\n   - **A** suggests funding would be denied if duplication \u226425%, which isn't necessarily true because the number of people could still be \u226550.\n   - **B** claims no other departments would have high duplication, which isn't supported by the context.\n   - **C** states fewer than 50 people, which would prevent funding, contradicting the university's decision.\n   - **D** correctly states that at least 50 people were available, allowing the department to be funded despite duplication.\n\n**Answer: D**",
