ing, you can't pass, so attending is necessary. This is similar to option B but phrased differently.\n\nNow, considering the context, the argument is that Xiaozhuang must attend because they're preparing for the exam. The options that address necessity are B and D. Option B is more about the necessity of attending for those who intend, while D is about the necessity of not attending if you can't pass. Both could be valid, but perhaps D is more directly addressing the necessity because it's about the implication that without attending, you can't pass, hence attending is necessary.\n\nAlternatively, option B is about only those who intend attending, which might mean that Xiaozhuang is among those who intend, hence must attend. But perhaps the stronger point is that without attending, you can't pass, which would make D the better answer.\n\nWait, but the argument is that Xiaozhuang must attend because they're preparing for the exam. So the argument is that attending is necessary. The options that support this are those that show that attending is required. Option B says only those who intend will attend, which might mean that Xiaozhuang is among those who intend, hence must attend. Option D says if you don't attend, you can't pass, which is a contrapositive of the necessity.\n\nI think both B and D are possible, but perhaps D is more directly addressing the necessity because it's about the implication that attending is required. Alternatively, perhaps B is better because it's about the necessity for those who intend.\n\nWait, perhaps the best way is to consider that the argument is that Xiaozhuang must attend because they're preparing for the exam. So the argument is that attending is necessary. Therefore, the options that support this are those that show that attending is required.\n\nOption B says only those who intend will attend, which might mean that Xiaozhuang is among those who intend, hence must attend. Option D says if you don't attend, you can't pass, which is a contrapositive of the necessity.\n\nI think the best answer is D because it directly states that attending is necessary, as without attending, you can't pass, hence attending is required.\n</think>\n\nThe best option to strengthen the argument is:\n\nD) If you do not attend the English tutoring class, you will not be able to pass the postgraduate entrance examination.\n\nThis option directly addresses the necessity of attending the class, as it states that without attending, you cannot pass, thereby reinforcing the argument that attendance is required.\n\n**Answer: D**",
