ds to better exercise, implying that cereal causes better exercise. But the context only shows that eating cereal (like cereal) leads to more exercise, not that eating cereal causes better exercise. So it's a correlation without causation.\n\nTherefore, the flawed argument is that it infers a cause from a mere correlation, which is option B.\n</think>\n\nThe argument in the advertisement is flawed because it infers a cause (better exercise) from a mere correlation (eating cereal leads to more exercise), without establishing causation. This is a common logical fallacy known as inferring causation from correlation.\n\n**Answer: B**",
