itions, but how does it relate to studying the undersea environment? It might be more about the physical conditions rather than the study itself. The context is about the amount of knowledge needed, not the conditions. So perhaps this is not the best option.\n\nD) Few people have seen detailed maps of undersea extensions, even though they exist. This directly relates to the study of the undersea environment. If many people don't know how to map the undersea, it implies that the undersea environment is less studied than land. That seems to fit the context.\n\nSo putting it together, option D supports the idea that undersea study is less than land because fewer people know how to map it, implying that the undersea environment is less studied.\n\nI think the correct answer is D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is D. This option supports the idea that the undersea environment is less studied than land by indicating that fewer people can map it, implying a lack of knowledge in that area.\n\n**Answer: D**",
