ing, leading to self-depletion. But the context says self depletion occurs during long-term work, and here it's about changing companies, which might not directly relate to work-related activities. Maybe this isn't the best fit.\n\nD) Wang has been working as an archive administrator for 15 years. He feels numb to his work and has no desire to change the status quo and get promoted.\n\nThis seems like Wang is working in a job where he's not motivated to change or improve, leading to a decline in ability or willingness to control the environment. This fits the definition of self-depletion because it's about feeling numb to work and wanting to stay in the same status quo, which could mean reduced control or willingness to make choices.\n\nSo, putting it together, option D seems to best fit the self-depletion phenomenon as described.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is D.\n\n**Answer: D**",
